use warnings;
use strict;

=head1 NAME

linux_entity - snmpd pass_persist for ENTITY-MIB, ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB, and ENTITY-STATE-MIB on Linux

=head1 VERSION

0.1.0

=cut

our $VERSION = '0.1.0';

=head1 SYNOPSIS

linux_entity B<-u> [B<-d> <cache dir>] [B<-i> <regexp>]... [B<-I> <regexp>]...
[B<--hwmon>] [B<--hwmon-kinds> <kinds>] [B<--rapl>] [B<--power>] [B<--inventory>] [B<--state>]

linux_entity B<-p> <base OID> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

linux_entity B<-w> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Reports pluggable transceivers, SFP, QSFP, and the like, as found via
C<ethtool -m>, as ENTITY-MIB inventory and their digital optical monitoring
readings as ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB sensors. Optionally also reports hwmon
sensors, RAPL power usage, power supplies and batteries, hardware
inventory, and ENTITY-STATE-MIB state.

It works in two parts. Ran with B<-u> from cron as root, it gathers
everything and writes the cache. Ran with B<-p> by snmpd, it answers
requests from the cache. snmpd never waits on ethtool, which can take
seconds per port on some drivers and needs root.

=head1 SNMPD CONFIG

    # cron
    */5 * * * * root /etc/snmp/extends/linux_entity -u --hwmon --rapl --power --inventory --state

    # snmpd.conf
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.47 /etc/snmp/extends/linux_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.47
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.99 /etc/snmp/extends/linux_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.99
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.131 /etc/snmp/extends/linux_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.131

One line is needed per MIB as they are in separate trees. B<-p> is told
the tree as snmpd does not pass along what it was registered for. The
.1.3.6.1.2.1.131 line is only needed with B<--state>.

Run B<-u> once before restarting snmpd so the cache exists. Without a
cache, nothing is returned.

Check that snmpd does not already answer for these first, as some builds
might. This should return nothing...

    snmpwalk -v2c -c public localhost .1.3.6.1.2.1.47

=head1 REQUIREMENTS

ethtool, plus a NIC and driver that support reading the module EEPROM for
transceivers. Not all do. Readings only show up for modules that support
digital optical monitoring.

=head1 FLAGS

=head2 -u

Update the cache. Needs root, as C<ethtool -m>, RAPL, and the SMBIOS
tables all do. Transceivers are always checked. The rest are added with
the flags below.

=head2 --hwmon

Add the sensors under /sys/class/hwmon. Temperature, voltage, current,
power, fan, and humidity are reported. This covers CPU temperatures,
NVMe drives, SATA drives with the drivetemp module loaded, GPUs, and
whatever else has a hwmon driver.

With B<--power>, hwmon devices that belong to a power supply are skipped
as those are already reported by it.

=head2 --hwmon-kinds <kinds>

Only report these kinds of hwmon sensors, as a comma separated list of
temp, in, curr, power, fan, and humidity. in is voltage and curr is
current. Defaults to all of them. Implies B<--hwmon>.

Mainly for keeping out what LM-SENSORS-MIB already reports, see
L</LM-SENSORS-MIB>.

=head2 --rapl

Add the CPU package, core, uncore, and DRAM power usage from the RAPL
zones under /sys/class/powercap. The kernel only provides a energy counter,
so the power is the average since the last run. Nothing is reported for
a zone until the second run.

=head2 --power

Add the power supplies and batteries under /sys/class/power_supply, with
their voltage, current, power, and temperature when present.

=head2 --inventory

Add the motherboard, CPUs, memory, and disks to the inventory. The CPUs
and memory come from the SMBIOS tables, so are not present on systems
lacking them. Disks are SATA, SAS, and NVMe, skipping removable ones.

=head2 --state

Add ENTITY-STATE-MIB state for the transceivers and, with B<--power>, the
power supplies.

=head2 -p <base OID>

Run as a snmpd pass_persist for the tree under the base OID.

=head2 -w

Print the cache the same as snmpwalk would, for debugging.

=head2 -d <cache dir>

The cache directory. Defaults to /var/cache/linux_entity. The cache is
entity.json in it, with state.json holding what is needed between runs.

=head2 -i <regexp>

Only check interfaces with a name matching this regexp for transceivers.
May be used more than once, in which case an interface matching any of
them is checked.

=head2 -I <regexp>

Skip interfaces with a name matching this regexp. May be used more than
once. Checked after B<-i>.

Only interfaces with a device under /sys/class/net/$interface/device are
checked to begin with, so virtual ones such as bridges, veth, and VLANs
are already skipped.

=head1 LM-SENSORS-MIB

If snmpd was built with lm-sensors support, it already serves the
temperature, fan, and voltage readings from the same hwmon chips under
LM-SENSORS-MIB, and LibreNMS picks those up. B<--hwmon> then reports them
a second time.

The better fix is hiding LM-SENSORS-MIB from LibreNMS and letting
B<--hwmon> replace it. LM-SENSORS-MIB names a sensor by its label alone,
so two sockets each have a "Core 0", its indexes are just positions that
shift if the chips are found in a different order, and LibreNMS does not
use its current or power readings. Add a excluded line to the view
LibreNMS polls through in snmpd.conf...

    view all    excluded  .1.3.6.1.4.1.2021.13.16

That is for the view named all from the snmpd.conf example in the
LibreNMS docs, so goes after its "view all included .1" line. If a
different view is used, use its name instead. The old lmsensors sensors
go away in LibreNMS and the hwmon ones are new, so their history does not
carry over.

If snmpd.conf can not be changed, leave out the kinds LM-SENSORS-MIB
already covers instead...

    linux_entity -u --hwmon-kinds curr,power,humidity

=head1 ENTITIES

Indexes are kept the same between runs, so a sensor or part keeps its
history.

- 1 :: The chassis, using what is in /sys/class/dmi/id.

- $ifindex * 1000 :: A transceiver port. entAliasMappingIdentifier maps it
  to IF-MIB::ifIndex.$ifindex.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 1 :: The transceiver. Vendor name, part number,
  revision, and serial go in entPhysicalMfgName, ModelName, HardwareRev,
  and SerialNum.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 10 :: Transceiver temperature, celsius.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 11 :: Transceiver voltage, voltsDC.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 100 + $lane :: Laser bias current, amperes at milli scale.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 200 + $lane :: Tx power, other with dBm as the units.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 300 + $lane :: Rx power, other with dBm as the units.

- 1000000000 and up :: Everything else. hwmon device numbers, disk names,
  and the like change between boots, so each is given the next free index
  the first time it is seen, keyed by something stable such as the device
  path. That is saved in state.json. Removing it renumbers everything.

$lane is 0 for single lane transceivers and the channel number for multi
lane ones, such as 1 to 4 for QSFP. Interfaces with a ifindex over 999999
are skipped so the transceiver indexes stay under 1000000000.

The transceiver Tx and Rx power sensors are named "$interface Tx Power
Sensor" and "$interface Rx Power Sensor", or "$interface Lane $lane Rx
Power Sensor" and so on for multi lane ones. ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB lacks a
dBm type and LibreNMS uses those names to treat them as dBm. A power
reading of zero, shown by ethtool as -inf dBm, is reported as -40 dBm.

Alarm and warning thresholds are not reported as ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB has
nowhere to put them. Battery charge is not reported either, as it lacks a
percent type.

=head1 STATE

With B<--state>, the entStateTable has a row for each transceiver and
each power supply.

- Transceivers :: entStateAdmin is unlocked if the interface is up and
  locked if down. entStateOper is enabled. entStateAlarm has critical set
  if any of the alarm flags are on and warning if any of the warning flags
  are on.

- Power supplies :: entStateOper is enabled or disabled going by online,
  for those that have it. entStateAlarm has critical set for a battery
  with a capacity level of critical, warning for one that is low, and
  major for any health other than good.

The rest of the columns are unknown.

=head1 INSTALL

Debian...

    apt-get install ethtool libjson-perl libfile-slurp-perl

Generic...

    cpanm JSON File::Slurp

=cut

